From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Sun, 12 Oct 2026 15:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: the division and two float helpers in assembly

The division helpers were the generic C in libgcc's own udivmodsi4.c
and udivmodhi4.c, and on this machine that is expensive twice over.
The algorithm shifts the divisor up until it passes the dividend and
then down again, so it runs two passes of as many steps as the two
numbers differ in magnitude; and it works on four 32-bit values, which
on six 16-bit registers do not fit -- the disassembly is full of
`cmp 16(sp),12(sp)' and `asl 20(sp)', two words of instruction and two
memory cycles apiece.

The assembly here does one pass of exactly thirty-two steps with the
dividend and the quotient in the same register pair (the dividend
leaves R1 at the top as the quotient's bits enter at the bottom), the
remainder in R4:R5 and the divisor in R2:R3: six registers, nothing
spilled.  Measured on the machine, ticks per operation including the
call:

  __divsi3    21769 -> 8491     __udivhi3    7137 -> 2556
  __modsi3    21722 -> 8589     __umodhi3    7170 -> 2568
  __udivsi3   31630 -> 8627     __negsf2     6072 ->  715
  __umodsi3   31651 -> 8594     __extendsfdf2 20040 -> 1007

The last two are the same kind of saving for a different reason.  In the
DEC F format negation is the sign bit flipped and widening to the D
format is the same two words with two zero words under them; fp-bit
takes the number apart into sign, exponent and fraction and puts it back
together to do either.

Those numbers are for the operands in tests/pdp11-fis, and the C ones
depend on their operands much more than the assembly does: __umodsi3 of
0xffffffff by 7 costs 31651 against 8594, but 1000 by 7 costs 12253
against 8409, since the normalisation pass is shorter the closer the two
numbers are.

What is deliberately left in C:

  - the comparisons, though the same hand-written set has them and they
    are twice as quick.  This format calls every number with a zero
    exponent a zero whatever its fraction, and -0.0 equal to 0.0; a
    comparison that works on the words themselves answers neither
    correctly, and tests/float catches exactly that.
  - float to integer (__fixsfsi, __fixunssfsi, __fixdfsi).  They agree
    on everything in range and differ on what is not: the assembly
    answers zero where libgcc saturates or wraps.
  - integer to float (__floatsisf, __floatunsisf).  Above 2^24, where
    the fraction no longer holds the value, the two round differently.
  - __floatdisf, which answers zero for 1LL; and __truncdfsf2, which
    truncates where libgcc rounds; and the four double-precision
    operations, which in that file compute with the top two words only
    -- a double with anything in its low words comes back wrong.

The code comes from this project's own stdlib/float/pdp11-fis.s, with
one bug fixed on the way: the signed remainder took its sign from the
low word of the dividend instead of the high one, so it came back
negated for a positive dividend whose low word happened to have bit 15
set -- `98304 % 7' answered -3.  examples/spigot, which is a long loop
of signed remainders, printed negative digits of pi until that was
found.

Checked: tests/float, math, fileio, rdwr and lseek all pass, the
examples build and run, spigot's digits are pi's, and
tests/pdp11-fis/verify.c -- 295 results from 22 routines over operands
picked at the edges of each -- gives the same answers as the library
gave before, except for the eight signed-remainder cases that were
wrong before.
---
 libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-divmod.S    | 152 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-fpmisc.S    |  64 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-udivmodhi.S |  90 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 libgcc/config/pdp11/t-pdp11           |  34 +++++++++--
 4 files changed, 333 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff -urN a/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-divmod.S b/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-divmod.S
--- a/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-divmod.S	2026-10-12 15:30:00
+++ b/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-divmod.S	2026-10-12 15:30:00
@@ -0,0 +1,152 @@
+/* 32-bit integer division for the PDP-11.
+   Copyright (C) 2026 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
+
+This file is part of GCC.
+
+GCC is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it under
+the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by the Free
+Software Foundation; either version 3, or (at your option) any later
+version.
+
+GCC is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY
+WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or
+FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the GNU General Public License
+for more details.
+
+Under Section 7 of GPL version 3, you are granted additional
+permissions described in the GCC Runtime Library Exception, version
+3.1, as published by the Free Software Foundation.
+
+You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License and
+a copy of the GCC Runtime Library Exception along with this program;
+see the files COPYING3 and COPYING.RUNTIME respectively.  If not, see
+<http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.  */
+
+/* The machine's own DIV divides 32 bits by 16 and is signed, so
+   anything wider or unsigned is a subroutine.  The four entry points
+   below share one loop: thirty-two shift-and-subtract steps, with the
+   dividend and the quotient in the same register pair -- the dividend
+   leaves R1 at the top as the quotient's bits enter at the bottom --
+   the running remainder in R4:R5 and the divisor in R2:R3.  Six
+   registers, nothing spilled, and no normalisation pass, which is what
+   makes this several times faster than the generic C in udivmodsi4.c.
+
+   The ABI is the compiler's own for a two-word type: the arguments are
+   on the stack, high word first, and the result comes back in R0:R1
+   with the high word in R0.  A word of operation type is pushed by the
+   entry point: bit 0 asks for the remainder rather than the quotient,
+   bit 8 says the operation is unsigned.
+
+   Division by zero yields zero.  C leaves it undefined and the machine
+   has no trap for it.  */
+
+	.text
+
+	.globl ___divsi3
+	.globl ___modsi3
+	.globl ___udivsi3
+	.globl ___umodsi3
+
+/* On entry, past the pushed type word:
+     14(sp) dividend high   16(sp) dividend low
+     18(sp) divisor high    20(sp) divisor low  */
+
+___divsi3:
+	clr	-(sp)		/* quotient, signed */
+	br	.Ldivmod
+
+___modsi3:
+	mov	$1, -(sp)	/* remainder, signed */
+	br	.Ldivmod
+
+___udivsi3:
+	mov	$256, -(sp)	/* quotient, unsigned */
+	br	.Ldivmod
+
+___umodsi3:
+	mov	$257, -(sp)	/* remainder, unsigned */
+
+.Ldivmod:
+	mov	r2, -(sp)
+	mov	r3, -(sp)
+	mov	r4, -(sp)
+	mov	r5, -(sp)
+	mov	$32, -(sp)	/* step counter */
+
+	mov	16(sp), r1	/* R0:R1 = dividend */
+	mov	14(sp), r0
+	bpl	1f		/* positive */
+	tstb	11(sp)		/* unsigned? */
+	bne	2f
+	neg	r1		/* no: take its absolute value */
+	adc	r0
+	neg	r0
+	incb	1(sp)		/* and remember to flip the quotient */
+	br	2f
+1:	bne	2f		/* high word is not zero */
+	tst	r1
+	beq	9f		/* dividend is zero: the answer is zero */
+2:	mov	20(sp), r3	/* R2:R3 = divisor */
+	mov	18(sp), r2
+	bpl	3f
+	tstb	11(sp)
+	bne	4f
+	neg	r3
+	adc	r2
+	neg	r2
+	incb	1(sp)
+	br	4f
+3:	bne	4f
+	tst	r3
+	beq	9f		/* divisor is zero: the answer is zero */
+4:	clr	r4		/* R4:R5 = running remainder */
+	clr	r5
+
+5:	asl	r1		/* dividend out at the top, quotient in */
+	rol	r0
+	rol	r5		/* the bit that left enters the remainder */
+	rol	r4
+	cmp	r4, r2		/* remainder >= divisor? */
+	blo	7f
+	bne	6f
+	cmp	r5, r3
+	blo	7f
+6:	sub	r3, r5		/* yes: subtract and set the quotient bit */
+	sbc	r4
+	sub	r2, r4
+	inc	r1
+7:	decb	(sp)
+	bne	5b
+
+	tstb	10(sp)		/* quotient wanted? */
+	beq	8f
+	mov	r5, r1		/* no: the remainder is the answer */
+	mov	r4, r0
+	tstb	11(sp)
+	bne	10f		/* unsigned: it has no sign to fix */
+	tst	14(sp)		/* signed: the remainder takes the sign of
+				   the dividend, whose high word this is --
+				   not 16(sp), the low one, which says
+				   nothing about the sign of the pair */
+	bpl	10f
+	neg	r1
+	adc	r0
+	neg	r0
+	br	10f
+8:	rorb	1(sp)		/* quotient: flip it if the signs differed */
+	bcc	10f
+	neg	r1
+	adc	r0
+	neg	r0
+	br	10f
+
+9:	clr	r0
+	clr	r1
+
+10:	tst	(sp)+		/* drop the counter */
+	mov	(sp)+, r5
+	mov	(sp)+, r4
+	mov	(sp)+, r3
+	mov	(sp)+, r2
+	tst	(sp)+		/* and the operation type */
+	rts	pc
diff -urN a/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-fpmisc.S b/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-fpmisc.S
--- a/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-fpmisc.S	2026-10-12 15:30:00
+++ b/libgcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-fpmisc.S	2026-10-12 15:30:00
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+/* 32-bit integer division for the PDP-11.
+   Copyright (C) 2026 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
+
+This file is part of GCC.
+
+GCC is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it under
+the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by the Free
+Software Foundation; either version 3, or (at your option) any later
